leafClassT -- OBJECT MODEL STROKE 1b (F-O23): A LEAF'S PARSE METHOD IS WHAT THE RULE IMPLIES, NOT WHAT
THE FIRST REFERENCE WALKED SAYS.

setParseWalk used to classify a leaf from the reference it happened to reach first -- reading that
reference's { } (rStuff.overTo) -- and install the answer on the rule's SHARED body, where every other
reference then used it. With Limit's plain `rightBrace-` walked first, SetBrackets' `rightBrace}` lost
its up-to on the new road ([abc] 0/0); with Limit gone, Braced's `rightBrace` gained one ([1] 1/2).
After the stroke the rule's method is rule-level (rightBrace is a literal: parseString), and the
dispatch (driveStep, runLeafParse) asks the REFERENCE first: its own { } means parse up-to.

